[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF on the Distribution Switch A pair, specifically the OSPF adjacency over VLAN 99 (br0.99) between dswa1 and dswa2. Mechanism: dswa1 briefly originated OSPF Hellos on br0.99 with a /32 netmask (taken from the anycast gateway VIP 10.0.0.1/32) instead of the subnet mask /28 of the SVI. dswa2 rejected these Hellos with “NetworkMask mismatch,” dropping the adjacency and triggering a short routing reconvergence. Evidence: dswa2’s FRR log repeatedly reports “Packet 10.0.0.79 [Hello:RECV]: NetworkMask mismatch on br0.99… configured prefix length is 28, but hello packet indicates 32” at 00:25:15, :26, :36, :46, :56, :06, before finally forming Full at 00:25:48. On dswa1, br0.99 carries both 10.0.0.2/28 and 10.0.0.1/32 (anycast VIP), confirming the condition that can make the /32 become the OSPF primary source address. Localization: dswa1 br0.99 (VLAN 99) OSPF interface; neighbor dswa2 was correct but suffered the mismatch. Root cause: Misordering/selection of the /32 anycast gateway VIP as the primary address for OSPF Hellos on dswa1 br0.99, causing OSPF mask mismatch and adjacency flap. Corrective fix: Ensure the subnet address (10.0.0.2/28) remains primary and the /32 VIP is secondary, or explicitly set ip ospf network point-to-point on br0.99 (or run the dswa1–dswa2 OSPF adjacency on a dedicated P2P VLAN without /32 VIPs). The brief adjacency loss explains the short gateway outage; the “slightly longer path” observed afterward is consistent with OSPF reconvergence and changed ECMP/DR selection across the core. 
=== END ===